ent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. So technical competence, decision quality, leadership, but issues with feedback and organization.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills. So reliability, problem solving, responsibility, but arrogance and weak leadership.\n\nWe need overall best fit. Which candidate balances criteria? Let's weigh.\n\nReliability: A 100% reliable, B conscientious, D takes responsibility, C unknown reliability.\n\nStress resilience: B handles stress well, D responds adequately, A unknown, C unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: C sees complex connections, spatial vision, leadership; D solves problems, concentrates; B computer skills; A quick decisions.\n\nCrew cooperation: A creates positive atmosphere, but egocentric and communication difficulty; B uncooperative, grumpy; C leadership but unorganized, not good at criticism; D arrogant, weak leadership.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously, A reliable, B conscientious, C leadership.\n\nOverall, candidate A has strong reliability and positive crew atmosphere, but communication difficulty and egocentric may hinder cooperation. However, positive atmosphere suggests good crew cooperation despite egocentric? Might be contradictory.\n\nCandidate B strong stress and conscientious but uncooperative.\n\nCandidate C strong technical and leadership but unorganized and poor at criticism.\n\nCandidate D strong problem solving and responsibility but arrogant and weak leadership.\n\nWhich is best? For long-distance pilot, technical competence and stress resilience are crucial, plus crew cooperation. Candidate C seems strongest technically, anticipates danger, sees complex connections, leadership. Concerns: unorganized, not good at criticism. Could affect safety? Unorganized may affect flight planning, but leadership may mitigate. Not good at criticism could affect feedback loops.\n\nCandidate A has reliability and quick decisions, but communication difficulty could be serious in cockpit.\n\nCandidate B stress and conscientious but uncooperative.\n\nCandidate D problem solving and responsibility but arrogance.\n\nI think candidate C is strongest overall for technical competence and decision quality, which are critical for long-distance. The concerns are manageable with training. So I will recommend Candidate C.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: none, but we can say \"Given the facts we have so far\". Provide evidence: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, leadership, but note concerns.\n\nThus vote for Candidate C.\n\nNow produce output."}
